Z||000000||Pretender||06-27-99||11:53 PM||||i've had this problem as long as i can remember but when i went on a trip to north carolina and back it seemed to have cured my hard starting and high ideling. but it has gone back to it's own ways again. i have pulled the console after the check engine light keeps coming on and it reads the idle air control valve.,. i replaced it with a new unit and nothing new, it still idels high(1500rpm) and i'm still getting the same code, could the new unit be bad as well? it's got me stumped. a friend said to unhook the battery and let it sit for an hour to reset the ecu does this help?.and could all of this be a symptom of my cracked exhaust manifolds??.:o(<br>
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||06-28-99||12:51 AM||||Yes, that will probably help. The ECM should <b>always</b> be reset after something has been changed to the engine - it needs to re-adjust its parameters. Unplugging the battery for an hour is a bit long. Officially 30 seconds should be enough but to be on the safe side, unplug it for 5 minutes or so. Drive the car normally for the first ten miles or so after resetting (so no full acceleration etc.).
Z||000002||Pretender||06-28-99||01:33 AM||||ok, i'll try it before work tomorrow. i hope it works out ok...thanks...
Z||000003||mwbackus||06-28-99||02:29 AM||||The symptoms you describe are also very likely to be caused by a vacuum leak or cracked EGR tube.  The fact that you are still setting a 35 indicates that this is very probable.  Replacing your PCV valve is also a good idea when high idle situations are present.  If you cannot visually determine a vacuum leak is present after inspecting the lines for cracks, etc., a can of carb cleaner can reveal this problem.  Spray a stream around the throtle body and  EGR tube and listem for a change in IDLE speed.  I have used starter fluid in the past since it will detect even the smallest of vacuum leaks, but I would caution anyone who does this that it is extremely flamable and could easily result in no more car.
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||06-28-99||10:51 AM||||Mwbackus is right. I overlooked the part in your message about the error code (so much for speed-reading).<p>Still, resetting the ECM is always necessary after a change has been made to the engine/sensors/etc.
Z||000005||theogre||06-28-99||02:52 PM||||there is a single orange wire with a disconnect in it near the terminal block next to the battery. Disconnecting the wire will clear ECM codes. I'm not sure the wire is there on all years. Beats the heck out of fooling with the battery if it is.
